  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SAFEGUARDING WINTERING HABITAT FOR RUFA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The northwestern coastline of Brazil hosts millions of shorebirds, having been identified as the third most important area for wintering shorebirds in South America including rufa Red Knot, Whimbrel, and Semipalmated Sandpiper. This area is also highlighted as the primary wintering area for the &apos;North Coast of South America&apos; Recovery Unit of rufa Red Knot. Multiple protected areas have been designated along this coastline, but most are in management categories with the least restrictions on use (e.g. extractive reserves, environmental protection areas) and have limited active enforcement or shorebird-specific management. Furthermore, with an expanding space flight launch site, the development of new ports, and the potential installation of wind energy facilities, shrimp farms and oil exploration, it is essential to better understand and safeguard the critical habitats for shorebirds in this region. The project will compile existing data to identify, delimit, and prioritize key areas for safeguarding and enhanced management. An aerial survey with a Red Knot specific protocol and increased International Shorebird Survey monitoring will add to the existing data to better understand the current status of shorebirds in the states of Para and Maranho. With the critical sites better defined, a stakeholder engagement process will be undertaken to define existing management structures, assess threats, identify opportunities for conservation action, and make recommendations regarding integrating shorebird needs within protected area management plans.</narrative>
  </description>
